   "html_text": "Related to the classic paradoxes of logic and set theory is the Paradox of the Stone. One begins by granting the basic dilemma, as an evident instance of LEM: either God is omnipotent or God is not omnipotent. With omnipotence, He can do anything, and in particular He can create a stone, call it ⟦M⟧ , that is so heavy even He cannot lift it. But then there is something He cannot do, viz. (ex hypothesi) lift ⟦M⟧ . But this is a violation of LNC: God can lift ⟦M⟧ and God cannot lift ⟦M⟧ . This par",
   "md_text": "Related to the classic paradoxes of logic and set theory is the Paradox of the Stone. One begins by granting the basic dilemma, as an evident instance of LEM: either God is omnipotent or God is not omnipotent. With omnipotence, He can do anything, and in particular He can create a stone, call it ⟦M⟧ , that is so heavy even He cannot lift it. But then there is something He cannot do, viz. (ex hypothesi) lift ⟦M⟧ . But this is a violation of LNC: God can lift ⟦M⟧ and God cannot lift ⟦M⟧ . This par"
